type TileGeometryKnowledge = SpatialKnowledge<Nat, String, String, String, String>

data tile_geometry_standing_note: String = "SPATIAL-1 CONSUMED 2026-08-11 after review. Every dimension in this carrier was called drawing intent in prose and carried as a plain Nat, which made a hypothesis structurally indistinguishable from a measurement - and the generated page duly drew hypotheses as solid filled bars with a footer saying nothing was measured. A footer is not epistemic safety. Tile geometry now enters as SpatialHypothesis, and the renderer reads it through project_spatial_rendering, so the arm it matches IS the standing and it cannot draw a hypothesis as a measurement without matching an arm that says so. When a tile is finally cut and measured, the same value arrives as SpatialMeasured and the drawing changes appearance without the renderer changing at all. That is the property worth having."

fn tile_drawing_intent(v: Nat) -> TileGeometryKnowledge {
  SpatialHypothesis { value: v, purpose: "machine tile drawing intent - no tile cut, nothing measured" }
}

fn scenario_depth_layers(s: MachineTileScenario) -> List<TileGeometryKnowledge> {
  [
    tile_drawing_intent(v: s.wall_to_backboard_face_mm),
    tile_drawing_intent(v: backboard_thickness_mm),
    tile_drawing_intent(v: s.rear_service_depth_mm),
    tile_drawing_intent(v: s.carrier_thickness_mm),
    tile_drawing_intent(v: s.tile_plane_to_front_tip_mm),
  ]
}

data machine_tile_design_note: String = "Rev A of the wall-mounted machine tile, authored as a program because the prose/drawing form had begun to contradict itself. Provenance: operator review 2026-08-10 raising six defects against the tile concept document (grip section unreadable, T-nut aligned to nothing, cleat seating not visually obvious, PSU model and whole-build wattage unstated, per-board cable interface and routing absent, resin grips doubling as bench feet), plus the routed root-cause synthesis of the same date. This carrier is the AUTHORITY for the tile's interface contract, datum chain, cable inventory, and power budget; the rendered concept document projects from it and never restates a number it does not carry. SCOPE: one tile and the wall it hangs on. It does NOT own site admission (strategy.site_model), the fabric-wide physical derivation (strategy.compute_fabric_model), or any hardware catalog row (extdeps.* in the public tree) - it consumes those authorities' shapes by reference and mints nothing they already name. SEED DEBTS, recorded not silent: (1) every dimension is a DRAWING INTENT, not a cut part - no tile has been made and no measurement exists; (2) the PSU is specified as a class with a loading target, not a purchased model with a receipt; (3) WITHDRAWN AND REPLACED: this debt previously read 'the ALTRAD8UD-1L2T power-header inventory (one 24-pin plus two 8-pin EPS12V) is read from the vendor page and is pending pin-verification'. Both halves were false. The board has no 24-pin socket - it carries three 8-pin ATX12V inputs and a 4-pin Micro-Fit signal connector - and no vendor page was ever read: every outbound fetch from this environment is refused by egress policy. The debt is now that the REQUIRED POPULATION of those three inputs is unresolved in extdeps, so no on-tile DC harness may be counted or drawn; (4) the lift mass is estimated by component, never weighed; (5) no thermal observation exists for a board mounted vertically on a panel - the vertical orientation is asserted to help the tower cooler, not measured."

data revision_supersession_note: String = "SUPERSESSION, stated rather than performed silently: the immediately preceding concept document placed each tile's power supply in a 300 x 300 mm floor-level service plinth and routed 24-pin ATX plus two 8-pin EPS runs up the wall to the board. That placement is RETRACTED by this carrier. It was reached from a hazard intuition (keep every mains connection at ankle height) and not from the derivation, and it fails three doctrines already ratified in strategy.compute_fabric_model: it creates a custom cable harness per tile with no commodity equivalent (no unearned relationship), it moves the DC boundary outward from the supply's own sealed enclosure to a wall-spanning run at tens of amps with no zero crossing to extinguish an arc (minimum local boundary), and it doubles the disconnect ritual from three connectors to five or six while making two of them energized DC (service access). The plinth SURVIVES as a real object with a smaller job: it carries the branch-circuit receptacles and the network switch, which have to be somewhere, and it is the drop point for every tile cord. It no longer carries a power supply."

data root_cause_note: String = "ROOT CAUSE of all six reviewed defects, recorded once: the document was carrying TWO incompatible tile-interface contracts and switching between them without saying so. Contract A puts the supply on the tile, so the tile presents three external connectors (mains inlet, data, BMC) and the removal ritual is unplug-three-and-lift. Contract B puts the supply off the tile, so the tile presents data, BMC, front panel plus however many DC inputs the board requires - a count this carrier can no longer state, because the board's required 12 V input population is unresolved. Every downstream defect is a symptom of the unresolved fork: the cable interface could not be drawn because it was two different interfaces; the wattage could not be stated because the supply had no fixed home; the grip could not be datumed because a bolted-through hold on a flat panel face has no natural datum - the load path is a fastener through a sheet, related to no edge; the grip fell into service as a bench foot because nothing else was declared to carry the tile at rest; the cleat was not legible because nothing had been asked to make it so; and the tile stayed rectangular because no feature had earned a shape. Rev A selects Contract A and makes every other element subordinate to it."

type MountBase
  = FreestandingBackboard
  | DirectWallTiles
  | FurnitureIntegrated

data mount_base_note: String = "FreestandingBackboard: a plywood field on its own feet, leaned and anchored to the wall, with the cleats cut into the field. Selected because the site is LEASED (strategy.site_survey_91_e21st, no lease signed) - DirectWallTiles puts a structural fastener pattern into someone else's 1911 masonry and creates a make-good obligation at exit, which is a dependency the derivation never asked for. The backboard also lets the whole wall of machines be built, wired, and thermally observed before the lease decision, which the site kernel's evidence-before-commitment posture wants."

type MountInterface
  = FrenchCleatPlain
  | CleatWithLowerLocators
  | ZClipHardware

data mount_interface_note: String = "A plain French cleat is one 45-degree rip and is correct on load. It is WRONG on legibility, which is the reviewed defect: a plain cleat is seated and unseated by feel, and a tile that is 90 percent seated looks identical to one that is fully seated. CleatWithLowerLocators keeps the single saw cut and adds the affordances enumerated in cleat_affordances - the seating state becomes readable at arm's length."

type CleatAffordance
  = SeatedReveal
  | LeadInChamfer
  | WitnessMark
  | LowerLocatorPin
  | BottomBumper

data cleat_affordance_note: String = "CORRECTION 2026-08-11, operator review: the earlier form of SeatedReveal derived the 6 mm shadow line FROM the cleat mate, which is geometrically incoherent - a French cleat seats by sliding down its own 45-degree plane, and down that plane is also inward, so a tile cannot sit 6 mm lower than its cleat without also sitting 6 mm deeper into the backboard, which it cannot. The reveal is therefore made independent of the mate. SeatedReveal: a 6 mm groove routed the full length of the backboard, its LOWER edge on the seated tile's top-edge line. Seated, the groove reads as a continuous dark line above every tile; a tile that is not home rides UP the bevel and covers its own length of groove, so the line goes MISSING at that position rather than merely changing width. LeadInChamfer: a 3 mm chamfer on both mating bevel arrises so the last 10 mm happens by gravity rather than by fitting. WitnessMark: a painted 8 mm stripe on the backboard below the cleat, covered by the tile only when seated. LowerLocatorPin: two 8 mm dowels on the backboard entering 10 mm counterbored holes in the tile's lower rail - they carry no load, they stop the tile swinging and they force square seating. BottomBumper: two 25 mm premium rubber pads at the tile's lower rail, thickness matching the cleat band exactly so the tile hangs parallel to the backboard instead of leaning on its bottom edge."

type TileShape
  = RectangularTile
  | AsymmetricLiveEdge

type EdgeTreatment
  = StraightDatum
  | GripCut
  | LiveEdgeGripCut

type TileEdge {
  name: String
  treatment: EdgeTreatment
  note: String
}

data tile_edge_note: String = "The shape rule that makes an irregular tile safe to build: EVERY DATUM EDGE IS STRAIGHT, exactly one edge is expressive. The top edge is the cleat datum and the bottom edge is the service datum; both are machine-straight because every dimension in datum_chain is measured from one of them. The two vertical edges carry the lift grips, and the right-hand one is the live edge - the grip cut is placed to follow the natural waney line rather than fight it, which is what makes the irregularity read as intended rather than as a mistake. An irregular tile with an irregular datum is not a design, it is an unmeasurable object."

type GripRole
  = LiftLoad
  | ColourIdentification

type GripRealization
  = IntegratedEdgeGrip
  | BoltedResinHold
  | BoltedWoodenHold

type Grip {
  id: String
  role: GripRole
  realization: GripRealization
  edge: String
  contacts_bench_at_rest: Bool
  note: String
}

data grip_reasoning_retraction_note: String = "RETRACTED 2026-08-11. This carrier reasoned that a hold bolted through an 18 mm panel FACE has no natural datum, because its load path runs through a fastener into a sheet and relates to no edge - and concluded from that that lift load must move into a routed edge grip. THE INFERENCE DOES NOT HOLD. A bolt-on climbing hold supplies its own local frame: origin at the primary bolt axis where it meets the mounting face, z along the outward face normal, x toward the anti-rotation fixing. Its position on the tile is then an ordinary offset from the same straight datums the board keep-out uses. The T-nut was aligned to something perfectly concrete - the hold's primary fixation axis - and the real failure was that this carrier had never modelled the hold's mounting interface at all. I diagnosed a modelling gap of my own and blamed the concept for it, then presented the substitution as a derivation. That is the derivation doctrine run backwards: a part was SUBTRACTED because it could not be drawn, which is exactly what this project exists not to do. It also overrode an explicit operator request for climbing holds, demoting them to colour. IntegratedEdgeGrip survives as a comparison candidate; it is no longer the selected lift realization."

data hold_qualification_note: String = "A climbing hold may be SELECTED as the primary lift handle and still not be QUALIFIED for it. EN 12572-3 covers holds on artificial climbing structures; a machine tile differs in loading direction, carrying cycle, panel stock, edge distance and failure consequence, so vendor standing does not transfer. What must be qualified is the whole load path - hand, hold body, centre bolt, anti-rotation fixing, T-nut or backing, panel, carrier, cleat - on a fixture using the exact selected parts. Until that receipt exists the page must say PRIMARY LIFT USE - QUALIFICATION PENDING rather than implying the hold is proven."

data grip_note: String = "The reviewed defect - the T-nut is not aligned with anything - has a cause, not a drafting fix. A hold bolted through an 18 mm plywood FACE takes its load path through a fastener into a sheet; that load path is related to no edge of the tile, so there is no datum for the T-nut to be dimensioned from, and any position drawn for it is arbitrary. The resolution is to split the two jobs the holds were doing. LIFT LOAD moves into the tile edge itself (IntegratedEdgeGrip), where the datum IS the edge and the load path is continuous wood; no insert, no fastener, nothing to loosen. COLOUR AND IDENTIFICATION stay with bolted resin climbing holds, which are cheap, abundant, and available in every colour - and which are now free to be placed anywhere, because they carry nothing. This is the derivation answer, not a compromise: the climbing hold survives for the reason the operator actually wanted it (colour variety at commodity cost) and is relieved of the job it was bad at."

type BenchRest
  = GripActsAsFoot
  | SeparateHiddenFeet
  | NoRest

data bench_rest_note: String = "A grip that is also the bench foot is a wear surface: it is the part the tile is set down on, dragged across, and pivoted about, and it is also the part hands trust. SeparateHiddenFeet gives the tile four 12 mm PREMIUM RUBBER pads at the lower rail and the lower third of the vertical edges, so the tile stands on rubber whether it is laid face-up or leaned on edge, and the grips never touch a bench. Rubber rather than cork on operator direction 2026-08-11, and it is the better material on the merits: cork takes a permanent set under a point load and crumbles at a cut edge, where a moulded rubber foot recovers, grips a bench instead of sliding on it, and damps the buzz a running board puts into a panel."

type PowerArchitecture
  = LocalPsuPerTile
  | RemotePsuPerTile
  | SharedLowVoltageBusbar

type ExternalConnector
  = AcPowerInlet
  | DataEthernet
  | BmcEthernet
  | Atx24Pin
  | Eps8PinA
  | Eps8PinB
  | FrontPanelHeader
  | DcInput48V
  | CoolantSupply
  | CoolantReturn

data external_connector_note: String = "EXTERNAL means it crosses the tile boundary and must be undone before a tile comes off the wall. The count of these IS the removal ritual, and it is the number the whole concept was sold on. Atx24Pin, Eps8PinA, Eps8PinB and FrontPanelHeader are declared here so the Contract B variant can be constructed and measured by witness - they are the arms Rev A must NOT reach."

type CableEndpoint
  = TilePsuModularPanel
  | TilePsuMainsInlet
  | BoardAtx24Header
  | BoardEps8HeaderA
  | BoardEps8HeaderB
  | BoardIoDataPort
  | BoardIoBmcPort
  | PlinthBranchReceptacle
  | PlinthSwitchPort

type Conductor
  = MainsAc
  | LowVoltageDc
  | TwistedPairData

type CableRun {
  id: String
  from: CableEndpoint
  to: CableEndpoint
  conductor: Conductor
  crosses_tile_boundary: Bool
  routed_in_saddle: Bool
  length_mm: Nat
  note: String
}

data cable_run_note: String = "The reviewed defect - the cabling is left out, each board needs two 8-pins or whatever the handbook says - answered as an inventory rather than a sentence. Under Contract A the ATX and EPS runs still exist; they simply never leave the tile, which is why they stopped being drawn and why that omission was invisible. The ServiceSaddle is the part that makes them a design instead of a bundle: a 30 mm routed channel down the tile face, covered by a removable oak strip on two thumbscrews, carrying every DC run from the supply's modular panel to the board headers. Lifting the strip exposes the whole harness; nothing is cable-tied to anything."

type DatumEdge
  = TopCleatDatum
  | BottomServiceDatum
  | LeftSquareDatum

type DatumStep {
  id: String
  from_datum: DatumEdge
  feature: String
  offset_mm: Nat
  note: String
}

data datum_chain_note: String = "Every feature on the tile is dimensioned from one of three straight edges, and no feature is dimensioned from another feature. That is the whole discipline, and it is what the reviewed grip section lacked: a chain that stops at a datum cannot drift, a chain that stops at another part accumulates every error upstream of it. The live edge is deliberately NOT a datum - nothing is measured from it, which is exactly why it is free to be irregular."

type PowerBudget {
  board_count: Nat
  per_board_budget_w: Nat
  total_budget_w: Nat
  planning_ceiling_w: Nat
  per_tile_peak_w: Nat
  per_tile_psu_rated_w: Nat
  note: String
}

type BranchCircuit {
  id: String
  volts: Nat
  amps: Nat
  tiles_served: Nat
}

data power_budget_note: String = "Per-board budget is built from parts, not guessed: Altra Max M128-30 at 183 W rated TDP, eight DDR4 registered DIMMs at roughly 5 W each, baseboard plus BMC plus two 10 GbE PHYs at roughly 40 W, cooler fans under 10 W, one boot NVMe at 8 W - about 280 W in steady service. 350 W is the per-board BUDGET, carrying headroom for a heavier DIMM population and for the transient the regulators pull before the cooler responds. The supply is then sized so that peak lands near the middle of its efficiency curve rather than at its shoulder, which is why a 650 W unit is the answer for a 350 W tile and not oversizing."

type TileDesignRefusal
  = InterfaceContractSplit { declared_external: Nat, observed_crossings: Nat }
  | UndatumedFeature { feature: String }
  | GripCarriesBenchLoad { grip_id: String }
  | CleatSeatingNotLegible { missing_affordance: String }
  | DatumWebTooThin { measured_mm: Nat, minimum_mm: Nat }
  | PsuLoadingOutsideBand { rated_w: Nat, peak_w: Nat }
  | BranchCircuitUndersized { capacity_w: Nat, ceiling_w: Nat }

data refusal_note: String = "Refusal arms are the shape of the six reviewed defects, so that the defect class cannot recur silently in a later revision. InterfaceContractSplit is the parent of the other five - it is the one that was actually true of the reviewed document."

data design_selection_collapse_note: String = "COLLAPSED 2026-08-11 after review. DesignSelection and its four rows (rev_a_selection, contract_b_variant, contract_c_48v_air, contract_d_48v_liquid) were a SECOND configuration vocabulary standing beside MachineTileScenario. Two configuration systems for one design is the same class of fault as two interface contracts in one document - the fault this carrier was created to end - and it survived a full recut because both halves were internally consistent. MachineTileScenario is now the only configuration authority. What varied by preset (external_connectors) moved onto it; what did not vary (mount base, mount interface, tile shape, bench rest, cleat affordances, tile edges) became module facts, because a field identical across every row is a design fact and duplicating it invites the divergence the collapse exists to prevent. The rejected remote-supply fork is no longer a parallel row: RearFixedToSupport already models it, and scenario_boundary_crossings adds its 24-pin and two EPS crossings, so the fork stays MEASURABLE without a second vocabulary to hold it."

data tile_overall_width_mm: Nat = 640

data tile_overall_height_mm: Nat = 500

data tile_thickness_mm: Nat = 18

data tile_cable_void_mm: Nat = 45

data cleat_band_mm: Nat = 25

data cleat_band_note: String = "The cleat is ripped from 25 mm stock, not from the tile's own 18 mm, and the two halves interlock inside ONE 25 mm band - so the seated tile's back face stands exactly 25 mm off the backboard, and the lower bumper is 25 mm for the same reason. Two consequences worth stating because the earlier drawing got both wrong. First, the 45 mm cable void is between the BACKBOARD and the wall, not between the tile and the backboard; nothing about the bumper sets it. Second, the mating plane rises as it comes away from the backboard, never toward it: sliding down that plane is also sliding inward, which is the entire reason a French cleat pulls a tile tight instead of levering it off. Drawn the other way it looks similar and falls off the wall. 25 mm of stock gives 25 mm of vertical mate at 45 degrees, which is what carries the 8 kg."

data minimum_datum_web_mm: Nat = 25

data lift_mass_estimate_kg: Nat = 8

data dimension_note: String = "640 x 500 x 18 mm birch ply, and the width is arithmetic rather than taste. Across: 60 mm left margin (of which the grip cut takes 32) + 244 mm board + 33 mm web + 30 mm service saddle + 33 mm web + 150 mm supply + 90 mm right margin (of which the grip cut takes 32) = 640. Down: 90 mm cleat zone + 58 mm exhaust clearance + 267 mm board + 85 mm cord drop and service datum = 500. The board is mounted with its rear I/O edge facing DOWN, so both cords leave at the service datum and neither crosses a grip. Estimated lift mass about 8 kg: 3.9 kg of ply, 1.9 kg supply, 1.3 kg board and memory, 0.6 kg cooler, 0.5 kg cabling, feet, saddle and holds - a two-handed lift for one person, which is the number the grip geometry is designed against, and an estimate by component that has never been weighed."



data grips: List<Grip> = [
  Grip {
    id: "grip-left-candidate",
    role: ColourIdentification,
    realization: IntegratedEdgeGrip,
    edge: "left",
    contacts_bench_at_rest: false,
    note: "32 mm deep, 110 mm tall, routed with a 12 mm roundover on both faces so the edge that takes the hand is a cylinder, not a corner. Load path is continuous 18 mm ply back into the field - no insert, no fastener, nothing to check on a service visit.",
  },
  Grip {
    id: "grip-right-candidate",
    role: ColourIdentification,
    realization: IntegratedEdgeGrip,
    edge: "right",
    contacts_bench_at_rest: false,
    note: "Same section, positioned to follow the live edge. Its exact position varies tile to tile because the live edge does; its HEIGHT above the service datum does not vary, so both hands are always level.",
  },
  Grip {
    id: "hold-lift-left",
    role: LiftLoad,
    realization: BoltedResinHold,
    edge: "tile-face",
    contacts_bench_at_rest: false,
    note: "RESTORED as the selected lift interface after the reasoning above was retracted. One exact climbing-hold SKU used at both hand positions, colour varying by power domain but geometry and mounting identical - every additional hold geometry is another extdeps row, another scan, another replacement SKU and another proof-load qualification. The part is modeled in extdeps.climbing_holds.atomik, which owns its identity, material, fixation interface and local frame; this carrier owns only role, pose and relation to the tile. Shape is UNKNOWN until a vendor STEP/STL arrives or a sample is scanned, so nothing may render a silhouette for it.",
  },
  Grip {
    id: "hold-lift-right",
    role: LiftLoad,
    realization: BoltedResinHold,
    edge: "tile-face",
    contacts_bench_at_rest: false,
    note: "The second hand position. Same SKU, same mounting, mirrored about the tile centreline so both holds sit at one height above the service datum and the tile lifts level. Its fixing DOES need a datum, and it has one: the hold's own frame places the bolt axis, and the drill point is that axis projected through the panel.",
  },
]

// Two sentences were deleted from the note above and are recorded here because deleting them
// silently would hide a contradiction this carrier shipped. They read: "It carries no lift load,
// so its fixing needs no datum", and a proposal to move the hold off the tile onto the backboard
// to carry colour only. Both are artefacts of the retracted reasoning, and the first directly
// contradicted the role: LiftLoad standing three lines above it. A declaration that argues against
// its own field is worse than either version alone, because whichever a reader believes, the model
// agreed with them.
data hold_note_contradiction_retraction: String = "The lift hold's note previously asserted it carries no lift load while its role field said LiftLoad. The note was the stale half; the role is correct. A bolt-on hold's fixing has a datum - the hold's own frame - which is precisely the point the earlier retraction established."

data datum_chain: List<DatumStep> = [
  DatumStep {
    id: "d-cleat-bearing",
    from_datum: TopCleatDatum,
    feature: "cleat bearing line where the 45-degree mate meets the tile cleat back face",
    offset_mm: 25,
    note: "The 45-degree rip meets the back face 22 mm down from the top edge. Everything about the cleat is this one number and the rip angle.",
  },
  DatumStep {
    id: "d-cleat-zone",
    from_datum: TopCleatDatum,
    feature: "bottom of the cleat zone - nothing may be mounted above this line",
    offset_mm: 90,
    note: "Keeps fasteners, saddle and board clear of the material the cleat load passes through.",
  },
  DatumStep {
    id: "d-board-top",
    from_datum: TopCleatDatum,
    feature: "board keep-out top edge",
    offset_mm: 148,
    note: "58 mm below the cleat zone, which is the clearance the supply's exhaust needs to leave the tile sideways before it reaches the board.",
  },
  DatumStep {
    id: "d-board-left",
    from_datum: LeftSquareDatum,
    feature: "board keep-out left edge",
    offset_mm: 60,
    note: "The number the web check is run against: 60 mm from the square left edge, of which the grip cut takes 32 mm.",
  },
  DatumStep {
    id: "d-grip-height",
    from_datum: BottomServiceDatum,
    feature: "grip cut centreline, both vertical edges",
    offset_mm: 180,
    note: "Set from the SERVICE datum, not the cleat datum, so that both hands sit at a fixed height above the surface the tile is set down on - the position that matters during a lift.",
  },
  DatumStep {
    id: "d-grip-depth",
    from_datum: LeftSquareDatum,
    feature: "grip cut depth into the edge",
    offset_mm: 32,
    note: "Four fingers to the second knuckle. Deeper starts eating the board keep-out web; shallower stops being a grip and becomes a scallop.",
  },
  DatumStep {
    id: "d-foot",
    from_datum: BottomServiceDatum,
    feature: "rubber foot centreline",
    offset_mm: 24,
    note: "Feet are on the service datum because that is the edge the tile is stood on.",
  },
  DatumStep {
    id: "d-saddle",
    from_datum: LeftSquareDatum,
    feature: "service saddle channel centreline",
    offset_mm: 352,
    note: "The DC harness runs down the right of the board, between the board keep-out and the live edge, where the tile is at its widest and the live edge is at its least predictable - the saddle cover is straight-cut and simply stops short of the waney line.",
  },
]

